ublicans were similarly affected by  information sources  in ongoing work we're exploring the  mechanisms driving these findings  so in summary at the individual level i  described several ways in which beliefs  can be changed that complement the  rational model of belief update  for instance belief endorsement can be  changed by manipulating mnemonic  accessibility  triggering prediction errors or making  normativity cues salient  so the next step in the social  interactionist approach is exploring how  such effects occur in dyadic contexts  so at this level we built on the  literature on social influence showing  how thinking is socially constructed  leading to an alignment between  interacting individuals  we wanted to see how the synchronization  might shape belief change  and to investigate we asked participants  to write a set of statements  after which we paired them in groups of  two and instructed them to discuss the  statements  lastly we asked participants to write  the statements again a posttest  and here on the x-axis i'll plot how  much the conversational partner endorsed  or opposed each statement in the  conversation  and on the y-axis how much the  participants changed their beliefs to  match what the partner had said  the results showed that participants  were very sensitive to their  conversational partners and increased  their beliefs in the statements their  partners endorsed  while decreasing their beliefs in the  statements their partners refuted  this pattern was strongest for initially  moderately held beliefs depicted here in  yellow  the takeaway of the study being that  individuals are sensitive  to their conversational partners  so finally to investigate how individual  cognitive effects propagate in social  networks  we  picked the memory impact of on beliefs  that i described in study one  and our paradigm at the collective level  mimics scenarios in which individuals  first listen to a public speaker  that reiterates some of their beliefs  and then communicate with one another  within their social networks about those  beliefs  to investigate the collective belief  dynamic of a group of people who  encounter such a situation  we invite participants into the lab in  groups of 12 to form a lab-created  social network  participants first evaluated the  believability of a set of health beliefs  organized across categories  after which some of the social networks  listened to a public speaker selectively  mentioning a subset of the previously  evaluated beliefs in the practice  condition  while the control networks filled out a  distracted task  and then all participants engaged in  conversational interactions within their  social networks following the temporally  unfolding conversational network  structure depicted here in four rounds  with each link between participants  depicting a dyadic interaction  so here we created a network that mimics  the main characteristics of real-world  social networks  clustered communities characterized by  frequent within cluster interactions  and lastly participants rated the  statements again and then a third time  after one week  we recruited a sample of 168 princeton  students to form 14 12-member  communities  and found that participants in the  practice networks synchronized their  beliefs with each other more than  participants in the control networks by  increasing their practice beliefs here  in orange but also by decreasing their  unpracticed beliefs here in blue  the finding that mnemonic accessibility  increases collective belief  synchronization made us wonder to what  degree the network structure that people  were embedded in mattered in how this  collective belief formed  so to investigate participants in  another study rated the accuracy of a  set of health statements after which  they were exposed to evidence either in  favor or against these initial  statements  and were then directed to the  conversational interaction phase in  which they had conversations with other  participants in their networks following  a temporary unfolding conversational  network structure in five rounds  lastly participants rated the statements  again  i recruited a sample of 140 princeton  students to form 14 10-member  communities  and to test whether the structure of the  network influenced the degree of  synchronization  we first constructed a hypothesis matrix  representing each network structure by  mapping for each pair of participants in  the network their distance from each  other in degrees of separation  we then computed the empirical data  matrix which contained the changing  correlations of beliefs for each pair of  participants  and write an unparametric statistical  test or bootstrap  to assess whether the empirical matrices  were better explained by the true  network structure than by any other  randomized network structure of  equivalent constraints that we simulated  and indeed found support for these  hypotheses meaning that belief  synchronization was determined by the  structure of the network that the  individuals were embedded in  in summary this research program can  issue a series of empirically driven  recommendations for policy makers  interested in changing beliefs in a  targeted fashion  for instance the strategy of increasing  mnemonic accessibility works best on  strongly endorsed beliefs  but different strategies worked better  at different levels of initial  endorsement  for instance a strategy that i didn't  discuss here but involves increasing  emotional arousal is most effective in  the case of initially strongly opposed  beliefs  and revealing the source worked best on  the case of moderately endorsed beliefs  so taking all of these findings together  to change a belief that is strongly  endorsed one could either increase its  mnemonic accessibility or trigger large  prediction errors  however if that beliefs is only  moderately endorsed the options are to  decrease its accessibility reveal the  information source or trigger large  prediction errors  and if the belief is initially strongly  opposed one could either associate it  with emotional images  emphasize social norms or trigger large  prediction errors  and if one engages in either of these  strategies at the level of the community  one could expect people's beliefs to  change to align with each other  conversations to amplify individual  processes  and people's beliefs to change according  to their network structure  so now let me give you a brief flavor of  some current research directions i'm  excited about  first i'm interested in understanding  the psychological drivers of climate  beliefs and climate action  this research stems from the newest  reports by the un warning that wildfires  floods drought and rising sea levels  will become more and more frequent when  you project projections showing that as  increasing parts of the world become  uninhabitable the displacements of  billions of people will create a refugee  crisis that could lead to worldwide  economic collapse  unless of course we engage in  coordinated global action  so in my current work i'm applying  sociocognitive strategies to stimulate  collective climate action  in an ongoing international  collaboration i'm comparing the  state-of-the-art collective climate  action interventions in a randomized  controlled trial conducted in over 80  countries around the world  in this experiment we're testing both  individual level climate action for  example tree planting efforts  as well as collective climate action  such as supporting climate policy  and these are the 11 interventions we're  testing which we chose in design with  input from our over 200 collaborators  and these are the projected relative  effectiveness as rated by our  collaborators  so we look forward to these um results  and to compare these predictions with  the actual results of the interventions  so stay tuned for these findings  projected to be collected by the end of  this year  in current work i'm also interested in  the role of artificial intelligence in  shaping our worldview beliefs  and this research trajectory is  motivated by the numerous instances of  algorithmic oppression  recently uncovered ranging from hiring  to university admissions the criminal  sentencing and healthcare allocation to  name a few  but what causes such biases  a common assumption is that they reflect  existing disparities existing in society  representing in the data on which these  algorithms are trained  however if this is the case there's a  risk that their outputs could propagate  these existing biases back into society  therefore thereby creating this  reinforcing bias of inequality  this perspective suggests that ai  decision tools could potentially  contribute to a cycle of perpetuating  inequality  to investigate this hypothesis we first  assess the relationship between existing  societal inequality and algorithmic  search output inequality in the context  of gender in two multinational studies  we first say the hypothesis that the  degree of gender inequality within a  society  would predict a pattern of algorithmic  gender inequality in internet search  outputs  and to investigate we obtained an open  source data set  known as the global gender gap index  computed by the world economic forum  containing rankings of gender inequality  for each country in the world  and to develop an assessment of gender  bias in algorithmic output we drew upon  classic research on the prototype bias  by which words such as person  student or human  are more often assumed to be a man  indeed recent research with using  national natural language processing to  analyze billions of words on the  internet also showed that the concept of  person  is more similar to that of men than to  that of women  and online algorithms to  designed to translate make gender biased  errors assigning a masculine gender to  professions such as doctor despite the  original sentence implying a woman  based on this research we developed an  approach which involved conducting  google image searches for the keyword  person  in each country using a vpn and  computing a gender bias score for each  country using the ratio of men to women  results in the first hundred images  using this approach should collect the  data in over 50 countries  and to assess our central hypotheses we  tested the relation between the global  gender gap on the x-axis and the degree  of gender disparity in these image  outputs on the y-axis and this effect  was highly significant such that the  proportion of male images was higher in  nations with greater inequality  in an initial sample of 37 countries and  a replication of 52 countries  therefore studies one and two provide  empirical evidence that societal level  gender disparities are reflected in  algorithmic outputs  having demonstrated this association  between gender inequality and  algorithmic output we turn to our next  question  whether exposure to such algorithmic  outputs can shape people cognitive's  concept and decisions in matters  consistent with this pre-existing  inequality thereby completing the cycle  of bias propagation  to investigate we recruited a sample of  u.s participants and presented them with  images depicting the google image search  results of new professions  and the gender composition of these  professions was manipulated to display  gender ratios of high inequality nations  for example approximately 90 men in  hungary or turkey  or low gender inequality nations with 50  50 men to women in iceland or finland  the procedure was the following first  participants made prototypicality  judgments regarding a new profession for  example they were asked who is more  likely to be a perker a man or a woman  and then participants were told they  would be able to see the google image  search results for these professions  and we randomly assign the professions  to appear in either the high or the low  inequality condition  as you can see here in the high  inequality condition  we  showed mostly men building wigs  and in a low inequality condition we  showed a ratio of one-to-one mental  women building wigs  and then we asked participants again  the prototypicality  question after which they were faced  with the first choice decision task in  which they had to choose between two  applicants for a job in each of the  professions that they learned about  here are the results plotted as the low  and high inequality conditions  against the proportion of women chosen  in the task and the 50 dollar line  represents no gender bias  at pre-test participants thought men  were more likely to perform the new  professions  and this replicates the male default  bias  i post this however per type ratings  became more egalitarian in the low  compared to the high inequality  condition  indeed exposure to search results  pattern found in the low inequality  nations reversed participants male bias  prototypes whereas exposure to search  results patterns found in the high  inequality nations appear to reinforce  and maintain this prototype  exposure to image sets in the low  inequality condition also produce a  higher likelihood of choosing women as  the job candidates  in a mediation analysis our overarching  prediction was supported exposure to  high versus low inequality search  results  shaped gender prototype formation which  in turn influence gender-biased hiring  in conclusion in two sets of  cross-national studies we provide  empirical evidence that societal  inequality is reflected in algorithmic  inequality  and that algorithmic inequality  influences decisions aligning with this  inequality further reinforcing the  pattern of discrimination in a  self-perpetrating cycle of bias  propagation  this finding suggests that new  intervention points for bias reduction  may involve uncovering how human  decision makers interact with  algorithmic outputs  together these research directions  aspire to advance our understanding of  some of the critical challenges facing  our society  growing inequality and the climate  crisis in my new lab at nyu we'll  continue focusing on these two  applications of collective cognitive  processes  and with that i would like to thank my  collaborators my funding sources and  more importantly all of you for  listening and i'm looking forward to  your questions so please email me if you  would like to further discuss  hi thank you for coming to my talk
 

